(* ============================================================================
   PART 10: HTML SERIALIZATION CONVENTIONS (NORMATIVE)
   ============================================================================

   These conventions define the exact bytes the corpus pins. On any
   disagreement the corpus wins.

   1. ATTRIBUTE ORDER. Attributes serialize in the AUTHOR's source order
      (the recorded `order`; see the RENDER ORDER rule in PART 4 and
      PART 9 §12-style note). An Attrs built programmatically with no
      recorded order falls back to a fixed `class`, then `id`, then
      key=value order. All classes accumulate (space-joined) into one
      `class` slot at its first-appearance position. An element with no
      effective attributes emits none. (A top-level heading's id moves
      to <section> and its other attributes do not, §13; with the
      `sections` option off, or for a heading inside a container, the id
      stays on the <h*> with the rest.) A mandatory base class (math `math inline|display`,
      a task list's `task-list`, §11) is prepended to that class slot.

      A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE LEADS. An attribute the element derives from
      its OWN marker or destination is not "generated" in the sense below:
      it is part of the element's shape rather than something added on top
      of what the author wrote. `<ol>`'s `type` and `start` (fixed by the
      first item's marker, §11 N2), a link's `href`, an image's `src` and
      `alt` are all structural. They are emitted BEFORE the authored
      attributes, and the authored order then follows unchanged:
        > {k=v .attr}  -> <ol type="a" k="v" class="attr">
        > a. alpha
      WHERE THE AUTHOR WROTE NO CLASS there is no slot to keep, and the
      position splits TWO WAYS. A MANDATORY BASE CLASS LEADS: math's
      `math inline|display`, an admonition's, `task-list`, and a core div's
      directive-kind class. A marker-derived class such as `line-block` or a
      diagram fence's class trails every authored attribute. An extension
      wrapper's `toc`, `glossary`, `index`, `tabs`, or `code-group` class also
      trails every authored attribute.

      DIRECTIVE CLASSES FOLLOW ELEMENT OWNERSHIP -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-011].
      With no authored class slot, a directive-kind class leads on a core
      `<div>` and trails on an element rendered by an extension. If the
      extension abstains, core div rendering applies. The index extension
      abstains when there are no terms, so an empty index renders
      `<div class="index" id="ix" k="v">`, while a populated index
      renders `<ul id="ix" k="v" class="index">`.

      The structural attribute examples match reference djot --
      `<ol type="a" class="attr">`, `<ol start="5" class="attr">`,
      `<a href="…" class="c" id="i" k="v">`, `<img alt="a" src="…"
      class="c">`, `<ul class="task-list c">` -- and it is the same
      placement PART 10 §1 already states for a mention, where the
      structural `href` precedes the author's `data-role` and `id`.
      SO THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THREE-WAY, not two: structural leads,
      authored follows in source order, engine-minted follows that.

      A GENERATED ATTRIBUTE JOINS AT THE END. The two rules above cover
      an all-authored Attrs and an all-programmatic one; the MIXED case
      is a generated attribute added to attributes the author wrote, and
      it takes the authored order first, then the generated attribute.
      In practice this is one attribute -- the auto-slug id an unwrapped
      heading carries (§13):
        > {a=b .c}     -> <h1 a="b" class="c" id="Auto">
        > # Auto
      An attribute the author WROTE keeps its authored position even
      when it is the id, because it is not generated:
        > {#x a=b}      -> <h1 id="x" a="b">
        > # Written
      So the discriminator is provenance, not the attribute's name: the
      author's own order is never rearranged, and anything the engine
      minted follows it. Pinned by the corpus.

      A RENDER ANNOTATION IS EMITTED LAST -- after the generated
      attribute, not merely after the authored ones. The one such
      annotation in the core is `data-source-line` (§13-adjacent, the
      `sourceLine` render option), which records where a block was
      written rather than describing the element:
        > ## Nested   -> <h2 id="Nested" data-source-line="1">
      This is a THIRD category, and stating it is what stops an engine
      from being conformant and divergent at once. An engine that
      appends the stamp at RENDER time gets the order for free, because the
      stamp is written outside the attribute walk; an engine that attaches it
      at PARSE time carries it inside the authored run, where the "generated
      last" rule alone would put the id behind it.

      TEXT-BLOCK ALIGNMENT IS A SEMANTIC ATTRIBUTE. On `<p>`, `<div>` and
      `<h1>` through `<h6>`, an authored `align` key whose trimmed,
      ASCII-case-folded value is `left`, `right` or `center` serializes as
      `style="text-align: VALUE;"`, not as the deprecated `align` attribute.
      The declaration takes the authored `align` slot. If the author also
      supplied `style`, emit one `style` attribute at its authored slot and
      append the declaration there; the appended declaration wins if that
      style already named `text-align`.

      The element and value sets are closed. On `<table>`, `align` means block
      placement rather than alignment of cell text, so it remains an ordinary
      attribute. Every element outside the text-block set, and every value
      outside the three named values, likewise keeps the authored attribute.
      Rewriting a table's placement as `text-align` would change the document,
      not modernize an equivalent spelling.

   2. ATTRIBUTE QUOTING + ESCAPING. Attribute values are double-quoted.
      In an attribute value escape `&`->&amp;, `<`->&lt;, `>`->&gt;,
      `"`->&quot;, `'`->&apos;. In text/content escape `&`,`<`,`>` (NOT
      quotes). ONE EXCEPTION: a NO-BREAK SPACE (U+00A0) in text content
      serializes as `&nbsp;`, the one entity beyond the three, so it
      renders visibly distinct from an ordinary space with no external
      CSS -- matching the escaped-space spelling `\ ` (PART 3) and a line
      block's preserved indentation (PART 9 §23), both of which already
      serialize the same way. No other entities are produced. This
      describes only the QUOTING of values that survive filtering:
      dangerous attribute names, values, and URL schemes are
      dropped/blanked FIRST per PART 9 §25.

   3. VOID ELEMENTS are written WITHOUT a trailing slash: `<hr>`,
      `<img …>`, `<br>`. A hard break serializes as `<br>` + newline.

   4. BLOCK WHITESPACE. Block elements are newline-separated, one per
      line. Nested block structures (list, blockquote, table, admonition,
      div, figure, section, endnotes) indent their children by TWO spaces
      per nesting level. Inline content stays flat on its block's line.

      AN EMPTY CONTAINER BODY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-001]. A container whose body renders
      nothing keeps a BLANK LINE where the body would be:

        <blockquote>

        </blockquote>

      This includes a bare `:::` div, a div with a word class, an admonition,
      a blockquote, a figure group, a line block and a local hard-break block.
      The container kind does not change the body slot. A body that renders to
      nothing is an empty body -- a container holding only a comment takes the
      same shape as one holding nothing. A rendered caption or div label is
      visible container content and therefore fills the body slot. PART 9 §23
      turns a comment-only line in a line block into an empty verse paragraph;
      that paragraph is visible body content (`<p></p>`), not an empty body.

      A MATCHING RAW BLOCK KEEPS ITS PLACEMENT SLOT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-012].
      A zero-line raw payload and one blank payload line are distinct under
      PART 2 raw_block. In a nested body, a matching raw block emits one line
      holding only the body's indentation when its payload has zero lines.
      That placement counts as visible content for [CARVE-P10-001] and
      [CARVE-P9-077]; it keeps a child slot beside a paragraph in a list item.
      A comment or a raw block for another target contributes no slot. These
      placement rules do not loosen a list; PART 9 §17 L1 governs tightness.

      A PLACED ELEMENT'S OWN TAGS CARRY THE PER-IMPLEMENTATION INDENTATION -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-010]. A `directive` (PART 12 §35) places an element around content the
      extension generates -- `<nav class="toc">`, `<dl class="glossary">`,
      `<ul class="index">`, a degraded `<div class="{kind}">`. The two halves are pinned
      differently.

      THE OPENING AND CLOSING TAG may carry the nesting indentation above or may sit at
      column 0. Both conform and neither is pinned, so a consumer comparing two
      implementations does not compare those two lines' leading whitespace. ONE COLUMN
      SERVES BOTH TAGS: an element whose opener and closer sit at different columns
      conforms under neither choice.

      THE GENERATED CONTENT between them is the cross-implementation contract,
      BYTE-IDENTICAL including its own leading whitespace. Where it is anchored is the
      extension's own statement rather than the marker's column: `::: toc` writes the
      `<ul>`, its items and the closing `</ul>` at column 0 wherever the marker was
      written, while a `glossary` row and an `index` item sit one level inside their own
      element and move with the column it took. A consumer compares the generated lines
      modulo that one column and nothing else.

        <section id="Heading">
          <h1>Heading</h1>
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
        <ul>
        <li><a href="#Heading">Heading</a></li>
        </ul>
        </nav>
        </section>

      `toc`, `glossary` and `index` are governed by this one wording. A kind's own
      section in `docs/extension-contract.md` fixes where its generated lines sit and
      may not redraw this boundary.

   5. PARAGRAPH WRAPPING follows the tight/loose rule (§17): a tight list
      item's text has no <p>; a loose item's paragraphs are wrapped.

   6. CODE. A fenced code block is `<pre><code[ class="language-X"]>` +
      escaped literal `code_block.content` + `</code></pre>` (PART 12 §3).
      No payload newline is added. An inline
      code span is `<code>` + escaped content + `</code>`.

      AN ESCAPED RAW BLOCK IS WRITTEN AS CODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-013].
      A raw block that a safe policy escapes rather than omits serializes
      exactly as a fenced code block whose language is the raw block's
      recorded `format`, verbatim. The escape is not a render loss; a policy
      that omits the block still reports `raw-format-dropped` ([CARVE-P2-024]).

        > ```=html      ->  <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;p&gt;raw&lt;/p&gt;
        > <p>raw</p>        </code></pre>
        > ```

   7. TABLES. Alignment renders as `style="text-align: VALUE;"` (one
      space after the colon, trailing semicolon); a cell with no effective
      alignment emits NO style attribute. `rowspan`/`colspan` are emitted
      only when their count is > 1.
      A visible resolved rowspan that crosses a row-group boundary cannot occupy its
      authored slots if those groups become separate HTML sections. In that
      case the whole table uses one `<tbody>` containing every row, in source
      order, instead of separate `<thead>`, `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` sections.
      Cell kind, `scope`, alignment and the resolved span count are computed
      from the original partition; only the HTML section wrappers change.
      This applies to derived and explicit partitions and to ListTable body
      groups. Tables with no crossing rowspan keep their sections.
      A ROW IS A ROW, IN EVERY SECTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-002]. `<thead>`,
      `<tbody>` and `<tfoot>` are each written on their own lines with ONE ROW
      PER LINE, indented one level inside the section:

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">a</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

   8. STABLE STRINGS. Mentions and tags render as inert `<span>`s (no link)
      when no URL template is configured; a configured template links to them. The footnote
      backlink glyph is `↩` (§16). Math wraps content in `\(…\)` /
      `\[…\]` inside `<span class="math inline|display">` (§18).

   9. SEMANTIC SPAN ATTRIBUTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-003]. On an ordinary `[content]{attrs}`
      span, three attribute names are reserved HTML rendering sugar: `abbr`,
      `time` and `kbd`. Each present name is consumed as an attribute and wraps
      the rendered inline content in its same-named element.

        `Press [Tab]{kbd} to indent.`        ->  `<kbd>Tab</kbd>`
        `[HTML]{abbr="HyperText Markup Language"}`
                                             ->  `<abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr>`
        `[now]{time="2026-01-01"}`           ->  `<time datetime="2026-01-01">now</time>`

      A non-empty `abbr` or `time` value becomes `title` or `datetime`; a value
      on `kbd` only selects the wrapper. A DERIVED ATTRIBUTE YIELDS TO AN
      AUTHORED ONE OF THE SAME NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-004]. `title` and `datetime` are
      ordinary attribute names an author may also write, so
      `[x]{abbr="gen" title="authored"}` is `<abbr title="authored">`, not two
      `title` attributes on one element. The author wrote that one in the
      output's own vocabulary; the derived one is a convenience for the case
      where they did not. Several names nest in this fixed
      INNER-TO-OUTER order, independent of the order the author typed them:
      `abbr`, `time`, `kbd`.

      LEFTOVER ATTRIBUTES RIDE THE OUTERMOST SEMANTIC ELEMENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-005].
      Where a semantic name is present, the span does not survive as a wrapper:
      every remaining id, class and key/value attribute lands on the outermost
      semantic element and passes through §1-2 plus PART 9 §25 hardening
      there.

        `[Tab]{#k .key kbd}`    ->  `<kbd id="k" class="key">Tab</kbd>`
        `[x]{#i time="12:00" kbd}`
                                 ->  `<kbd id="i"><time datetime="12:00">x</time></kbd>`
        `[x]{kbd onclick="e"}`   ->  `<kbd>x</kbd>`
        `[x]{}`                  ->  `<span>x</span>`
        `[x]{onclick="e"}`       ->  `<span>x</span>`

      PART 9 §25 is an ATTRIBUTE policy: it removes attributes
      and never the element the author wrote, so a span with no semantic name
      renders its `<span>` whether or not anything survives hardening, and
      corpus `108-security-hardening-8` and `-10` pin those bytes. A semantic
      name is not an attribute that was removed - it never reaches the output
      as one. It RENAMES the element the author wrote, and the author's
      attributes ride the element they were written on rather than a wrapper
      the author did not write.

      THE REGISTRY HOLDS NO ELEMENT CARVE ALREADY SPELLS INLINE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-006].
      A name is reserved only where the language offers no OTHER INLINE
      SPELLING for that element -- no second way to wrap a run of text where it
      stands. `code` and `mark` do not qualify: a code span (`` `x` ``) writes
      `<code>` and the highlight syntax (`=x=`) writes `<mark>`, each the same
      authoring gesture on the same run. (The inline literal `` !`x` `` is not
      a third spelling of `<code>`: §27 drops the wrapper, which is the point
      of it.) A code span is VERBATIM (`` `*b*` `` -> `<code>*b*</code>`),
      whereas `:code[*b*]` would parse its body and produce
      `<code><strong>b</strong></code>`, giving one tag two content models.

      THE TEST IS THE SPELLING, NOT THE TAG, and `abbr` is why it has to be
      said. A document-level `*[HTML]: …` definition also emits `<abbr>`, and
      that does NOT disqualify the name: a definition is a different mechanism
      at a different level. It expands EVERY occurrence of its term
      automatically and document-wide, and cannot mark one.
      `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}` marks exactly one, with its own title, while the
      definition keeps expanding the rest; `[WHO]{abbr="World Health
      Organization"}` marks a term no definition declares; `[HTML]{abbr}` marks
      an abbreviation with no expansion at all. None of those is writable with
      `*[…]:`.

      THE CORE SEMANTIC NAMES ARE CLOSED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-007]. A name
      is core when it preserves otherwise-lost authored data or a core clause
      already governs its interaction. Core recognizes exactly `abbr`, `time`,
      and `kbd`; `kbd` is also retained for its broad use across comparable
      systems. `samp`, `var`, `cite`, and `dfn` meet neither criterion and
      belong to the SemanticSpan extension described in §10.

      An explicit `abbr` attribute is authoritative over automatic abbreviation
      expansion inside that span. A resolved `abbreviation` child contributes
      only its visible abbreviation text there; renderers MUST NOT emit a
      nested `<abbr>`. Thus a document-level `*[HTML]: Long` definition does not
      override `[HTML]{abbr="Custom"}`.

      This rule changes only HTML rendering. The AST remains an ordinary `span`
      carrying the authored attributes, plain and ANSI targets render its
      inline content, and the canonical writer emits the span with its
      attributes, an empty-string one spelled bare per PART 11 §6c.

      Accessible ruby is NOT reserved here: PART 12 §32 gives it structured
      base/annotation children rather than a single inline container.

   10. THE OTHER SEMANTIC NAMES ARE AN EXTENSION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-008]. `samp`, `var`,
      `cite` and `dfn` carry no data and collide with no core clause, so they
      are not core: a conformant core renders `[x]{samp}` as an ordinary
      boolean attribute (`<span samp="">x</span>`) and `:samp[x]` through the
      generic `<span class="ext-samp">` fallback.

      They are the `SemanticSpan` extension, Tier-2 and off by default, whose
      contract is `docs/extensions.md` §8: when it is enabled the four names
      behave exactly as §9's three do, under the same nesting order, the same
      value mapping for `dfn`, the same attribute riding, and the same
      hardening. The extension accepts `:name[…]` for all seven names as a
      soft-deprecated form scheduled for removal in 0.2; writers use the
      compact spelling.

      A core implementation MUST NOT turn an arbitrary extension name into an
      HTML tag: outside `SemanticSpan`, every `:name[…]` keeps the generic
      fallback. `cite` denotes a work title or source and is unrelated to a
      bibliographic `[@key]` citation, which is the Citations extension's.

   11. A TASK LIST AND ITS NAMED TASK STATES NAME THEMSELVES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-009].
      PART 2 admits seven task states and §2's box has two
      positions, so `[-]`, `[_]`, `[>]` and `[?]` rendered bytes identical to
      `[ ]`: the distinction the author made survived the file (PART 11 §6g)
      and died at the renderer, out of reach of any stylesheet. The item
      carries `data-task-state` holding the authored character:

        - [-] dropped   ->  <li data-task-state="-"><input type="checkbox"
                            disabled aria-label="dropped"> dropped</li>

      A DONE ITEM NAMES ITS STATE TOO. `[x]` and `[X]` are one state, so both
      carry `data-task-state="x"`, always the lowercase `x`, and one selector
      (`li[data-task-state="x"]`) reaches a done item's text without `:has()`.
      AN OPEN ITEM STAYS BARE: `[ ]` carries no attribute. The AST does not
      change: `checked` records the done state and `taskState` stays absent
      for `[ ]`, `[x]` and `[X]` (PART 12), so the renderer writes `x` from
      `checked`.

      THE LIST CARRIES `task-list`. A task list's `<ul>` carries the
      mandatory base class `task-list` (§1): it leads when the author wrote no
      class and is prepended inside the authored class slot when one exists.
      PART 9 §11 N1 makes plain-vs-task a same-list axis, so a list is all
      task items or none and the class reaches every item. For an imported or
      supplied AST, derive this class only on a nonempty unordered list whose
      direct items all have a checkbox. Mixed and ordered lists gain no derived
      task-list class. An authored task-list class remains authored when that
      shape is absent. A plain list nested
      inside a task item is not a task list and stays bare:

        - [ ] open       ->  <ul class="task-list">
        - [x] done             <li><input type="checkbox" disabled
                               aria-label="open"> open</li>
                               <li data-task-state="x"><input type="checkbox"
                               checked disabled aria-label="done"> done</li>
                             </ul>

      ON THE `<li>`, NOT ON THE `<input>`. The state is a property of the
      ITEM -- PART 2 says so of the checkbox itself -- and a stylesheet
      reaching the item's own text is what the attribute is for
      (`li[data-task-state="-"]`). Putting it on the box would put the
      selector one level below the content it wants to reach.

      IT IS A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE and leads the authored ones (§1), like
      `<ol>`'s `type` and a link's `href`: the item derives it from its own
      marker rather than from anything the author attached. When a structural
      state is derived, suppress an authored attribute with the same
      ASCII-case-insensitive name so the item has one state attribute. Without
      a derived state, authored attributes retain their existing behavior.

      THE VALUE IS THE RECORDED CHARACTER, escaped as §2 escapes any value --
      `>` is written `&gt;`, and a value outside PART 2's enumeration is not
      one this clause writes.

      AN IMPORTER CONSUMES IT, and consuming is not the same as dropping a
      DERIVED attribute (PART 9 §16a). The checkbox `<input>` is already
      consumed into the item's state rather than kept as content; this
      attribute is the other half of that state and is read the same way. It
      is what lets a document survive a render-and-import cycle: without it
      `[-]` comes back as `[ ]`, silently, because both spellings render the
      same box and no diagnostic can see a difference that is not there.
      `data-task-state="x"` beside a checked box is the done state, consumed
      with the box rather than kept as an authored attribute, and the list's
      `task-list` class is consumed as a structural class: neither comes back
      in the imported source.

      THE ACCESSIBLE NAME DOES NOT MOVE. The box keeps the item's own text as
      its `aria-label` (PART 2). Naming the state there would invent copy the
      author did not write, and the states are an author's shorthand rather
      than a vocabulary this spec can translate.

   12. AN ORDERED LIST'S DELIMITER NAMES ITSELF ON THE LIST -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-014]. PART 2 admits `.` and `)` after an ordered marker and
      §1 carries only `type` and `start`, so `1)` and `1.` rendered identical
      bytes: the distinction the author made survived the file (PART 11 §6)
      and died at the renderer, out of reach of any stylesheet. The list
      carries `data-delim` holding the authored character:

        1) first   ->  <ol data-delim=")">
                         <li>first</li>
                       </ol>

      ONLY WHERE THE AST RECORDS A DELIMITER, which is the same rule PART 11
      §6 states for the field: a `delim` of `.` is absent there and carries no
      attribute here, because the default needs no spelling, and such a list's
      bytes are unchanged by this clause.

      ON THE `<ol>`, NOT ON THE `<li>`. The delimiter is a property of the
      LIST, fixed once by its first item's marker (§11 N2), so a nested list
      derives its own from its own marker and does not inherit the attribute.

      IT IS A STRUCTURAL ATTRIBUTE and leads the authored ones (§1), beside
      `<ol>`'s own `type` and `start`, which it trails: the list derives it
      from its own marker rather than from anything the author attached.

      THE VALUE IS THE RECORDED CHARACTER, escaped as §2 escapes any value,
      and a value outside PART 2's enumeration is not one this clause writes.

      AN IMPORTER CONSUMES IT, as §11 says of `data-task-state` and for the
      same reason: without it a rendered `1)` imports back as `1.`, silently,
      because both spellings rendered the same bytes before this clause. A
      value outside PART 2's enumeration is an author attribute, not a
      delimiter.


   13. ROUNDTRIP EXPORT WRITES NAMED CARRIERS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-015].
      §1 to §12 describe the bytes every render writes. A document also holds
      distinctions this serialization has no spelling for, and once a render
      drops one no import can recover it. ROUNDTRIP EXPORT is one opt-in that
      adds the carriers §13a names and changes nothing else:

        PHP renderer          setRoundTripMode(true)
        JavaScript options    roundtrip: true
        Rust options          roundtrip: true / with_roundtrip(true)
        CLI                   --roundtrip

      IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT on every surface. The default bytes are the ones
      §1 to §12 pin, and enabling the option neither removes nor reorders an
      attribute those sections write.

      THE TARGET DECIDES THE CARRIER. For HTML it is the names in §13a. For
      Markdown it is the container carrier comments of PART 11, which that
      target already writes under its own option; the shared flag SELECTS
      them rather than redefining them. A Markdown export carries containers
      and no more, and is not a claim to preserve what §13a lists.

      WHAT IT GUARANTEES IS RECOVERY OF THE NAMED DISTINCTIONS, not
      byte-identical source. A distinction the AST already records --
      `list.bulletChar`, `thematic_break.marker`, a link's or image's `ref`
      and `rawRef`, an `autolink` node, a `footnote_ref` label, an
      `escaped_text` node, a `raw_inline` format, `table.separatorWidths`, an
      authored `id` under `attrs` -- is recoverable from the tree whether or not HTML carried it.
      These carriers exist because this serialization collapses those
      distinctions, not because the tree lost them. A DISTINCTION THE AST DOES
      NOT RECORD GETS NO CARRIER, and §13a says why.

      WHAT IT DOES NOT PROMISE IS A RAW BLOCK. §13a names no carrier for one,
      so a raw block whose format this target cannot write is dropped under
      this option exactly as it is without it, and the render reports the
      dropped format as a diagnostic in both cases. ENABLING THE OPTION NEVER
      SUPPRESSES THAT DIAGNOSTIC: a preservation mode that loses a block
      silently is worse than a default one that says so. Whether a raw block
      should get a carrier at all is open, because carrying one means putting
      arbitrary foreign content into an attribute or a comment, and that is a
      security question this clause does not settle.

   13a. THE HTML CARRIERS ARE THE NAMES IN THIS INVENTORY -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-016]. Under roundtrip export, and only under it, the CORE
      HTML target writes these names and no other preservation name. Each row
      is the attribute, the node it rides on, and what the value holds:

        data-marker                 <ul>          the authored bullet marker,
                                                  written only where it is not
                                                  `-`
        data-char                   <hr>          the authored thematic-break
                                                  character, written only
                                                  where it is not `-`
        data-carve-explicit-id      <section>,    presence: the id was
                                    <h1>-<h6>     authored, not generated. It
                                                  rides the `<section>` where
                                                  PART 9 §13 wraps one, and
                                                  the heading where it does
                                                  not
        data-carve-ref              <a>, <img>    the reference label the link
                                                  or image resolved by
        data-carve-autolink         <a>           presence: the link was
                                                  authored as an autolink
        data-carve-footnote-label   <a>, <li>     the authored footnote label,
                                                  on the reference and on the
                                                  endnote item. The AST field
                                                  it restores is
                                                  `footnote_ref.label`, which
                                                  [CARVE-P12-047] names and
                                                  spells, not `id`
        data-carve-inline-footnote  <li>          presence: the endnote came
                                                  from an inline footnote
        data-carve-raw              <span>        the raw inline's format; the
                                                  span's TEXT holds its content
        data-carve-escaped          <span>        presence; the span holds the
                                                  one escaped character
        data-carve-abbreviations    <template>    presence; the template's TEXT
                                                  holds the document's
                                                  abbreviation definitions, one
                                                  `*[ABBR]: expansion` per line
        data-carve-col-widths       <table>       the authored separator-row
                                                  dash widths, comma-joined,
                                                  one per separator cell. The
                                                  AST field it restores is
                                                  `table.separatorWidths`
                                                  ([CARVE-P11-066]), whose
                                                  PRESENCE is the distinction:
                                                  a table with no separator row
                                                  writes no attribute

      AN ORDERED LIST'S DELIMITER IS `data-delim`, NOT A SECOND CARRIER. §12
      writes the authored delimiter on every render, and an ordered list's
      marker IS that delimiter: its style is `type` and its first number is
      `start`, both structural and both already written. An `<ol>` carrying
      both names says one thing twice, so `data-marker` rides a `<ul>` only.

      A CARRIER LEADS THE AUTHORED ATTRIBUTES. A carrier is structural by §1 --
      the element derives it from its own marker, destination or resolution --
      so it sits after the element's other structural attributes (`type`,
      `start`, `data-delim`, `href`, `src`, `alt`, `title`) and before the
      first attribute the author wrote:
        > {.c #i}
        > ***            -> <hr data-char="*" class="c" id="i">
      THE PLACEMENT DOES NOT VARY BY CARRIER. Trailing the authored attributes
      for one name and leading them for another is not a choice this clause
      leaves open.

      A CARRIER EXISTS ONLY FOR A DISTINCTION THE AST RECORDS. Every row above
      names a field or a node type PART 12 already carries, which is what makes
      this set DERIVABLE rather than hand-maintained: a reader checks it against
      the tree instead of trusting the list. THE WAY TO ADD A CARRIER for a
      distinction the tree discards is to add the PART 12 field FIRST, and then
      this clause follows.

      THE SEPARATOR-ROW ROW IS WHAT THE RULE ADMITTING A CARRIER LOOKS LIKE.
      `data-carve-col-widths` was absent from this inventory until
      `table.separatorWidths` existed, and it is listed now because that field
      does. The order was not a formality: an engine that wrote the carrier
      WITHOUT the field turned 37 tests red across 9 files, because the AST JSON
      writer does not filter to the published field set, the reader refuses an
      unknown field, and the "parses to the same tree" comparisons are deep
      equality that a canonical `---` separator cannot satisfy. The field
      answers all three at once, which is why it comes first.

      A CONSTRUCT'S VERBATIM SOURCE IS WHAT THE RULE STILL EXCLUDES, which is
      why `data-carve-src` is absent from the rows above. It is not awaiting a
      field: [CARVE-P11-013] rules the glued opener canonical and says the tree
      records no padding, fence length or fence character, so the one
      distinction such a carrier is observed to move -- the space in
      ``` ``` php ``` -- is a spelling this specification has already ruled
      against keeping. A field for it would reverse that clause rather than add
      to it.

      AN EXTENSION WRITES ITS OWN CARRIERS, under the reserved prefix, for the
      distinctions ITS nodes record. They are scoped to that extension and
      enumerated in that extension's own contract rather than here, which is
      what the CORE qualifier above leaves room for. AN EXTENSION CARRIER IS A
      CARRIER in every other respect: §13b displaces an authored attribute of
      its name, §13c reads its deprecated spelling, and §13d consumes it in
      every import mode.

      A WIDTH IS A POSITIVE INTEGER, AND A READER CLAMPS IT AT 1000. The
      widths arrive as a comma-joined list in an attribute an importer reads
      off UNTRUSTED HTML, and the writer spells one `-` per unit, so an
      unbounded value is a document-size amplification rather than a wide
      table. A reader clamps each entry into `[1, 1000]` and refuses the whole
      carrier -- writing no field at all -- where an entry is not a run of
      digits. THE BOUND IS STATED SO THE ENGINES AGREE ON THE BYTES: it is a
      round number chosen for parity rather than derived from anything, and no
      separator row anyone writes approaches it.

      A VALUE ESCAPES AS §2 ESCAPES ANY ATTRIBUTE VALUE. §2's text-content
      exception for U+00A0 is not an attribute rule, so a carrier value
      holding a no-break space keeps that character.

      A CARRIER THAT HOLDS ITS PAYLOAD AS CONTENT ESCAPES IT AS §2 ESCAPES TEXT
      CONTENT, and a reader takes the element's TEXT rather than its serialized
      markup. `data-carve-raw`, `data-carve-escaped` and
      `data-carve-abbreviations` are the three, and for `data-carve-raw` this is
      not a formatting preference: written unescaped, a payload of `<b>` opens a
      tag the span never closes and the rest of the paragraph disappears into
      it. Reading the serialized markup back is the same mistake from the other
      side, because it escapes the payload a second time and a raw inline
      holding `&` returns holding `&amp;`.

      A PRESENCE CARRIER CARRIES NOTHING IN ITS VALUE.
      `data-carve-explicit-id`, `data-carve-autolink`,
      `data-carve-inline-footnote` and `data-carve-escaped` say what they say
      by being there. A writer writes the empty value or `1`; a reader reads
      presence and does not compare the value.

      ONE ESCAPE PER ELEMENT. A `data-carve-escaped` span holds exactly ONE
      escaped character. A writer never collects a run of escapes onto one
      span and a reader never reassembles one, because a span holding `abc`
      would import as `\a\b\c`, which is not Carve. A span carrying more than
      one character is MALFORMED and the importer takes its content as text.
      Reporting it is permitted, not required: an importer that builds a tree
      without a diagnostic channel still has to read the span.

      THE RESERVED SET IS THIS ENUMERATION, NOT A PREFIX PATTERN. These names
      are the serializer's protocol rather than an author's content, and each
      one is reserved because this inventory lists it. So `data-marker` and
      `data-char` keep their own spelling and are no less reserved for
      carrying no prefix, and a `data-carve-` name this inventory does not
      list is NOT a carrier and NOT reserved: an engine does not invent one,
      and a reader treats it as an ordinary authored attribute. Adding a name
      to this inventory is a change to this clause.

      THE SPEC'S OWN ATTRIBUTES ARE NOT CARRIERS. A task list's `task-list`
      class and its items' `data-task-state` (§11), and an ordered list's
      `data-delim` (§12), are written by every render, with or without this
      option, and each has its own clause. This option neither gates nor
      duplicates them.

   13b. A CARRIER WINS A COLLISION AND THE DROPPED ATTRIBUTE IS REPORTED --
      NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-019]. Where a node carries an authored attribute
      whose name is a reserved name from §13a, THE CARRIER IS WRITTEN AND THE
      AUTHORED ATTRIBUTE IS DROPPED. A carrier is structural and leads the
      authored attributes by §1, and §12 already reads a value outside its
      own enumeration as an author attribute rather than as the spec's.

      THE DROP IS A RENDER-LOSS ROW NAMING THE ATTRIBUTE, so the document
      records the loss instead of taking it silently. The code is
      `carrier-attribute-dropped` and the row carries `attribute`, the displaced name;
      [CARVE-P2-024] states the rest of its shape.

      EMITTING BOTH IS NOT CONFORMING. Two attributes of one name is invalid
      HTML, and the precedence does not vary by carrier: the rule is the same
      for every name in the inventory. REFUSING THE EXPORT IS NOT CONFORMING
      EITHER -- one authored attribute cannot veto a mode the caller asked
      for.

   13c. AN IMPORTER READS THE DEPRECATED `data-djot-` SPELLING -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P10-020]. The prefixed names in §13a were spelled `data-djot-`
      before this clause, and documents exported by released engines carry
      that spelling. So, for every prefixed name in the inventory:

        A WRITER EMITS `data-carve-` ONLY. The `data-djot-` spelling is never
        written, under this option or any other.

        AN IMPORTER ACCEPTS EITHER SPELLING, and `data-carve-` wins where one
        element carries both. `data-djot-` is DEPRECATED: it is read for
        compatibility and consumed exactly as §13d consumes the canonical
        spelling, so a document that arrives carrying it leaves without it.

      THE ALIAS IS NOT A SECOND SET OF NAMES. A `data-djot-` name this
      inventory does not list under its `data-carve-` spelling is not a
      carrier and not reserved; it is an ordinary authored attribute.
      `data-marker` and `data-char` have no alias, having never carried a
      prefix.

   13d. EVERY IMPORT MODE CONSUMES THE INVENTORY AND WRITES NO RESERVED NAME
      BACK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-021]. Consuming a carrier reconstructs
      syntax the AST can already express, so it is not a trust decision and
      is not gated on an import mode. `safe` import consumes every name in
      §13a, in either spelling, exactly as `roundtrip` import does.

      A RESERVED NAME NEVER LEAVES AS AN AUTHORED ATTRIBUTE. An importer that
      consumed a carrier does not then write its name into the reconstructed
      document's attributes: `<hr data-char="_">` imports as `___`, not as
      `---{data-char=_}`, and `<a href="/u" data-carve-ref="lbl">text</a>`
      imports as `[text][lbl]`, not as `[text](/u){data-carve-ref=lbl}`. A
      reserved name an importer has no use for is still consumed rather than
      passed through, with or without a diagnostic saying so.

      ONE CARRIER IS THE EXCEPTION, and §13e is why. `data-carve-raw` RETURNS
      ITS PAYLOAD VERBATIM as a raw inline, so consuming it is not
      reconstructing syntax the tree already expresses -- it decides that
      foreign content may re-enter the document as raw. That stays on the
      importer's own trust setting, and an importer that declines it drops the
      carrier and takes the span's text.

      WHAT THE MODE DECIDES IS NOTHING ELSE. Every other name in §13a is
      consumed whatever the mode, and reconstruction of raw HTML outside a
      carrier is §13e's question rather than this clause's.

   13e. EXPORT DOES NOT SELECT AN IMPORT MODE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-017].
      Roundtrip export adds information to OUTPUT. HTML import's `roundtrip`
      mode decides reconstruction and raw-HTML preservation of INPUT, and
      finding one of these carriers in a document is not a reason to trust
      that document. Enabling export never enables a trusted import, never
      widens an importer's mode, and never relaxes a destination check. An
      importer that returns a stored-source payload verbatim does so on its
      own trust setting, never on the exporter's.

   13f. AN ALIAS NEVER TURNS PRESERVATION OFF -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P10-018].
      The Markdown carrier options that predate this one -- PHP
      `setCarryMarkers(true)`, JavaScript `carryMarkers: true`, Rust
      `with_carry_markers(true)`, CLI `--carry-markers` -- keep working with
      their current Markdown-only scope. Where the canonical option and a
      legacy alias can both be supplied, EITHER ENABLED OPTION ENABLES
      PRESERVATION, in either order: a default never overrides an alias the
      caller enabled. `carryMarkers` is not redefined as an HTML option. A
      narrower control, if one is ever wanted, is a separate explicit setting
      rather than a change to this rule.

   ============================================================================ *)
